Niko Home Control II Hobby API integration

Public setup and terms entrypoints:
https://www.niko.eu/en/our-products/home-automation/hobby-api
https://guide.niko.eu/en/connhc2/lv/hobby-api

Protocol researched against Niko Hobby API Technical Documentation v1.4,
25 March 2025, obtained read-only from the owner's public documentation URL:
https://mynikohomecontrol.niko.eu/content/hobbyapi/documentation.pdf
The document is marked confidential/shared under Niko terms. Neither that PDF,
its text, nor Niko CA certificates are bundled here. Owners supply their own
per-installation username/JWT and permitted CA certificate through Settings.

Implementation evidence (not a hardware validation claim):
- Pages 8-10: local MQTT TLS port 8884, supplied CA, username/JWT authentication.
- Pages 12-19: devices.list, nested Params/Devices arrays, PropertyDefinitions
  Description/HasStatus/CanControl, configuration-change events.
- Pages 20-22: devices.control has no success response, properties may partially
  apply, offline devices may emit no error/status, devices.status observation.
- Pages 23-26: locations.list and location topics; singular location/err appears
  in examples, so both singular and plural error topics are subscribed.
- Annex A pages 41-42: Type action / Technology nikohomecontrol / Model dimmer
  identifies a dimmer action. Status Choice(On,Off) and Brightness Range(0,100,1)
  are distinct; brightness zero is not Status Off.
- Annex A page 44: relay is a relay/circuit, not a proven safe lighting class.
  Relays, sockets, scenes, motors and unknown classes are always read-only even
  if their names resemble lights or they appear in an owner's allowlist.

Bounds: 32 KiB incoming JSON, four completed frame slots plus one assembly
buffer in PSRAM, 16 MQTT events, two serial worker requests, 64 devices,
32 locations, 16 properties per device, 20-device pages, <=8 KiB tool results,
16 owner-enabled UUIDs. Overflow/malformed framing stops admission and requires
explicit reconfiguration; no dropped final action evidence is called success.
One connection owns MQTT and one actor owns parsing/cache/actions. Reconnect
only subscribes and refreshes discovery; no write is retained, queued in an
application outbox, retried, or replayed. Writes use MQTT QoS0/non-retained once.

Requires a trustworthy device UTC clock for TLS/JWT expiry, a matching owner-
supplied TLS identity/CA, Hobby API activation with current Niko software,
same-LAN reachability, and durable SDK started acknowledgment for every write.
Controller evidence is not causal/physical proof. Cancellation after publish,
timeout, transport failure, and missing fresh evidence are uncertain outcomes.

Host entrypoint: bash components/niko_home_control/test/run.sh
This exercises the actual production parser/validator/outcome classifier on
synthetic vendor-shaped data. It does not exercise TLS, MQTT on the ESP32,
actual controller identity, household actions, SD durability, or firmware fit.
